    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 16 with a high-floor offensive profile, headlined by **Junior Caminero** (70.80) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (64.79). **Junior Caminero** remains our top play; despite a moderate park factor, his high base projection and favorable matchup against Alcantara (xERA 4.46) create a potent environment for run production. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** earns a strong start based on a massive 14% platoon boost; his speed remains our primary weapon in a category where we prioritize efficiency above all else. **Casey Schmitt** also presents a compelling case at 63.92, bolstered by a 10% park factor and the opportunity to exploit a shaky outing from Severino. Conversely, we are keeping a close watch on **Yordan Alvarez** (54.65); while his raw talent is elite, the presence of deGrom on the mound significantly suppresses his ceiling, forcing us to temper expectations despite his inherent power.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Hyeseong Kim** (47.96), who hovered near the \"Marginal\" threshold all morning. While **Hyeseong Kim** carries a respectable platoon advantage, his unfavorable spot in the nine-hole combined with the high defensive efficiency of the opposing starter ultimately pushed him behind the likes of **Gary Sanchez** and **Chase Meidroth**, who benefit from superior lineup placement. As for risks, we are monitoring the matchup for **Trea Turner** (59.13) and **Kyle Schwarber** (57.29) against Chandler; while both remain solid starts, the 4% SP skill penalty suggests they will need to work counts carefully to avoid negative outcomes. Finally, we remain clear of **Colt Keith**, **Christian Yelich**, and **Zac Veen**, who are currently confirmed out of the lineup or on minor league assignment, ensuring our limited daily games are reserved only for active, high-leverage contributors like **Kazuma Okamoto** and **Luke Raley**.",
